{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 30, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that delves into the origins of facebook with a sharp, intelligent script and relentless pacing. directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, the movie transforms what could have been a dry corporate origin story into a gripping psychological drama about ambition, betrayal, and the cost of innovation. jesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, capturing his brilliance, social awkwardness, and emotional detachment with chilling precision. his portrayal is not a caricature but a nuanced study of a man who built a global empire while remaining profoundly isolated.\n\nthe supporting cast is equally compelling. andrew garfield brings vulnerability and heart as eduardo saverin, zuckerberg’s best friend turned adversary, while jesse plemons and jordan belfort (yes, that jordan belfort) lend gravitas as the winklevoss twins, whose legal battle becomes both a narrative engine and a haunting mirror to zuckerberg’s moral ambiguity. the film’s dialogue is electric—fast, witty, and layered with subtext, a hallmark of sorkin’s writing. every line feels deliberate, every pause pregnant with meaning.\n\nfincher’s direction is cold and precise, using a muted color palette and tight framing to reflect the sterile, high-pressure world of tech startups and legal depositions. the score by trent reznor and atticus ross is hauntingly electronic, a perfect sonic embodiment of digital isolation and restless ambition. the editing is razor-sharp, seamlessly interweaving multiple timelines to build suspense and thematic resonance.\n\nbeyond the surface-level story of facebook’s creation, the film is a profound commentary on the nature of connection in the digital age—how technology promises to bring people together while often deepening loneliness. it questions whether genius justifies betrayal, and whether success can ever compensate for lost relationships. the final scene, where zuckerberg refreshes a facebook page over and over, hoping for a reply from his ex-friend, is one of the most chilling and poignant moments in modern cinema.\n\nthe social network is not just a movie about a website—it’s a tragedy about human connection in the age of algorithms. it’s smart, tense, beautifully acted, and devastatingly relevant. it doesn’t just entertain; it lingers, forcing you to question the cost of the world we’ve built.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00014625910262111574}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -2.0265558760002023e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -9.536738616588991e-07}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -4.410734163684538e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.00043156370520591736}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.4582989513874054}, {"token": "fully", "logprob": -0.252384752035141}, {"token": " crafted", "logprob": -0.0010349161457270384}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.003972972277551889}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00011789103882620111}, {"token": " del", "logprob": -1.1295183897018433}, {"token": "ves", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " into", "logprob": -0.006728847976773977}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " origins", "logprob": -0.5502323508262634}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -6.3774932641536e-05}, {"token": " facebook", "logprob": -0.0003295593778602779}, {"token": " with", "logprob": -0.39783698320388794}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -1.7515952587127686}, {"token": " sharp", "logprob": -0.3482113182544708}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.20241503417491913}, {"token": " intelligent", "logprob": -2.0077154636383057}, {"token": " script", "logprob": -0.09272413700819016}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.3138715922832489}, {"token": " relentless", "logprob": -0.32817018032073975}, {"token": " pacing", "logprob": -0.09916935116052628}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.005